  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms have developed over the \nBighorn Mountains this afternoon as a subtle shortwave trough \nmoves through the zonal flow aloft. With this, additional isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms are possible into the early evening \nover the Bighorn Mountains, as well as the Absaroka-Beartooth \nMountains. While the chance is low, a few thunderstorms may also \ndevelop over Carter County in far south-eastern Montana this \nafternoon and early evening. With this, a strong thunderstorm is \npossible (low risk), though the best thunderstorm ingredients \ncurrently exist over South Dakota. \n\nAs ridging builds into the region Friday, temperatures will warm \ninto the upper 80s and 90s for many locations. While dry \nconditions will prevail through much of the day, some weak energy \nmoving in from the southwest may be enough to generate high- based\nshowers and weak thunderstorms during the afternoon and evening \n(15 percent chance). With this, gusty and erratic winds may \ndevelop with dry air in place near the surface. \n\nSaturday through Monday, there continues to be high confidence in\nthe first widespread significant heat wave of the summer as a \nstrong upper level ridge builds into the region. Currently, \nforecast high temperatures of 95 to 110 degrees are forecast, \nwarmest on Sunday, with local maxima above 110 degrees possible \nover the plains (moderate chance). Temperatures this warm will \nthreaten all- time record high temperatures at all climate sites \non Sunday (see table below for all- time record high \ntemperatures). Temperatures this warm are supported by the very \nwarm temperatures aloft that are modeled during this time (700mb \ntemperatures near or exceeding 20C). Along with the significant \ndaytime temperatures, overnight low temperatures in the 60s and \n70s, warmest Sunday night, will exacerbate the risk of heat \nstress. Therefore, the Extreme Heat Watch is now in effect from \nSaturday morning through Monday evening. \n\nAlong with the heat, very dry conditions will exist, with \nrelative humidity values decreasing into the single digits to \nteens percent Saturday and Sunday, and teens to 20s percent \nMonday. On Sunday, a wind signal has started to appear in the \nmodels. Currently, peak wind gusts in the 20s and 30s mph are \nforecast Sunday into Sunday night. The combination of breezy \nwinds, very hot temperatures, and very dry conditions will \nincrease the risk of fire weather conditions. Take care not to \ncause a spark and follow local burn regulations. \n\nHeading into the first half of next week, ensembles suggest the \nupper level ridge will decrease in strength a bit, allowing \nslightly \"cooler\" temperatures to return to the forecast (upper \n80s and 90s). Monsoonal moisture also looks to lift from the \nsouthwest into northern Wyoming and southern Montana, bringing at\nleast an occasional slight chance of precipitation back to the \nforecast. With this, high based showers and thunderstorms would be\nfavored, meaning the main threat is gusty and erratic winds. \n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F",
